From 756d0a705679f3412269aa707ae5e88498ec8506 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:44:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: bq2415x: re-assert the regulation voltage in
 the timer work

Set once at probe, VOREG falls back to the chip's 4.20 V power-on default when
the safety timer expires (which it does whenever nothing is charging), so a
3.8 V battery the BSI detected would charge to 4.20 V the next time a cable is
plugged. Re-assert init_data's voltage on every timer tick - one i2c write, and
it keeps the charger configured across the chip's own timeouts.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/bq2415x_charger.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/bq2415x_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/bq2415x_charger.c
index a02013bbe..1ba351c88 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/bq2415x_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/bq2415x_charger.c
@@ -944,6 +944,17 @@ static void bq2415x_timer_work(struct work_struct *work)
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Re-assert the battery regulation voltage: without a running charge the
+	 * chip's safety timer expires and resets VOREG to its 4.20 V power-on
+	 * default, which would undercharge a 3.8 V battery the BSI detected. Cheap
+	 * (one i2c write) and it also keeps the charger configured as init_data
+	 * says across the chip's own timeouts.
+	 */
+	if (bq->init_data.battery_regulation_voltage)
+		bq2415x_set_battery_regulation_voltage(bq,
+				bq->init_data.battery_regulation_voltage);
+
 	boost = bq2415x_exec_command(bq, BQ2415X_BOOST_MODE_STATUS);
 	if (boost < 0) {
 		bq2415x_timer_error(bq, "Unknown error");
-- 
